<span class="kicker">2026-08-08 / RIDGEWELL GUIDES</span><h1>How Do You Install a Paver Walkway?</h1>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section article-section"><div class="wrap editorial-layout"><article class="prose blog-content"><p>Installing a paver walkway is a process most homeowners only go through once or twice in their lives, which means it’s easy to have questions about what actually happens, how long it takes, what order things get done in, and what a professional crew is doing at each stage that might not be obvious from the outside. This guide walks through what installation actually looks like when it’s handled by a professional team, from the first site visit to the final walkthrough.</p>
<p>At Ridgewell Landscape and Design, we install paver walkways across the Denver area, including Broomfield, Parker, and the surrounding communities, and one thing we’ve learned over the years is that homeowners feel a lot better about the process when they know roughly what to expect at each stage. If you’re planning to build a walkway yourself instead, our<a href="/blog/how-to-build-a-paver-walkway/"> how to build a paver walkway</a> guide covers the hands-on steps in detail. This one is written from the other side, what it looks like when someone else is doing the installing.</p>
<h2><b>The Installation Process at a Glance</b></h2>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Stage</b></td><td><b>What Happens</b></td><td><b>Typical Timing</b></td></tr><tr><td>Site assessment and design</td><td>Measuring, soil check, material and pattern selection</td><td>Before installation begins</td></tr><tr><td>Layout and marking</td><td>Walkway path marked on site</td><td>Day 1, morning</td></tr><tr><td>Excavation</td><td>Digging out the path to the correct depth</td><td>Day 1</td></tr><tr><td>Base installation</td><td>Gravel base added and compacted in layers</td><td>Day 1 to 2</td></tr><tr><td>Sand bed and paver laying</td><td>Bedding sand screeded, pavers placed</td><td>Day 2 to 3</td></tr><tr><td>Cutting and edge restraints</td><td>Border pavers cut, edges locked in</td><td>Day 2 to 3</td></tr><tr><td>Jointing and final compaction</td><td>Polymeric sand swept in, final compact pass</td><td>Final day</td></tr><tr><td>Walkthrough and cleanup</td><td>Final inspection, site cleanup</td><td>Final day</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Timing varies with walkway length, pattern complexity, and site conditions, but this gives a general sense of how the stages typically flow for a standard residential walkway project.</p>
<h2><b>Before Installation Begins: Site Assessment and Design</b></h2>
<h3><b>The Initial Site Visit</b></h3>
<p>A professional installer starts with an in-person site visit, walking the proposed walkway path, checking soil type and drainage patterns, and discussing what the walkway needs to connect to or accommodate. This is also when slope, existing hardscape, and any obstacles like tree roots or utility lines get identified before anything is finalized.</p>
<h3><b>Material and Pattern Selection</b></h3>
<p>Based on the site assessment and your design preferences, this stage covers choosing paver material, color, and laying pattern. It’s worth taking real time here, since this decision affects both the finished look and, in some cases, the installation timeline, more intricate patterns like herringbone generally take longer to lay than a simple running bond.</p>
<h3><b>Getting a Written Quote</b></h3>
<p>A clear, itemized quote should break out material costs, labor, base preparation, and any additional work like drainage improvements or removal of an existing surface. This is the point to ask questions and confirm exactly what’s included before work begins.</p>
<h2><b>Day 1: Layout, Excavation, and Base Work Begins</b></h2>
<h3><b>Marking the Final Layout</b></h3>
<p>Once work begins, the crew marks the exact walkway path on site using stakes and string or marking paint, giving you one last chance to see the layout in real dimensions before excavation starts.</p>
<h3><b>Excavation</b></h3>
<p>The crew excavates the marked area to the correct depth, accounting for base material, bedding sand, and paver thickness combined. Excavated soil is typically hauled away unless you’ve arranged to keep it for another use on the property.</p>
<h3><b>Base Material Delivery and Installation</b></h3>
<p>Crushed gravel base material is added in layers, generally around 2 inches at a time, with a plate compactor running over each layer before the next one goes down. For a typical residential walkway, this stage often starts on day 1 and can carry into day 2 depending on total length and depth requirements.</p>
<h2><b>Day 2 to 3: Sand Bed and Paver Installation</b></h2>
<h3><b>Screeding the Bedding Sand</b></h3>
<p>Once the base is fully compacted and confirmed level, a thin layer of bedding sand is screeded across the surface using guide rails, creating an even bed for the pavers to sit on.</p>
<h3><b>Laying the Pavers</b></h3>
<p>Pavers are placed following the chosen pattern, starting from a fixed edge and working outward. This is typically the most visually satisfying stage to watch, since the walkway starts taking its final shape here, though it’s also one of the more time-intensive steps, particularly for intricate patterns.</p>
<h3><b>Cutting Border Pieces</b></h3>
<p>Once the main field of pavers is down, the crew measures and cuts border pieces individually to fit the remaining edge gaps precisely, rather than relying on pre-cut estimates.</p>
<h3><b>Installing Edge Restraints</b></h3>
<p>Edge restraints are installed along both sides of the finished walkway and anchored with spikes, locking the entire installation in place against future shifting.</p>
<h2><b>Final Day: Jointing, Compaction, and Walkthrough</b></h2>
<h3><b>Sweeping in Joint Sand</b></h3>
<p>Polymeric sand is swept into the joints between pavers, with excess removed from the surface before the area is lightly misted to activate the sand’s binding properties.</p>
<h3><b>Final Compaction Pass</b></h3>
<p>A final pass with the plate compactor sets everything firmly in place, and the crew does a last visual and physical check for any pavers sitting slightly high or low.</p>
<h3><b>Site Cleanup</b></h3>
<p>A professional crew should leave the site reasonably clean, removing excess material, sweeping any residual sand or debris off nearby surfaces, and clearing equipment and packaging from the property.</p>
<h3><b>Final Walkthrough</b></h3>
<p>Before the crew leaves, walk the finished walkway together, checking for level, joint consistency, and confirming the edges and finish match what was discussed during design. This is the right time to raise any concerns, not after the crew has already left the property.</p>

